WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
Keanu Reeves' no longer really retired&nbsp;
assassin has killed hundreds of opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.440
when they screwed him over or got in his way.&nbsp;
There’s no stopping Baba Yaga. Here are some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.680
the hardest-to-watch, most memorably brutal&nbsp;
deaths in the "John Wick" series thus far.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.720
This is the death that set John Wick off on&nbsp;
his killing spree, and any animal lover can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.760
likely relate to such an urge. Underneath all the&nbsp;
glorious violence and destruction, this franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.600
is ultimately an ode to man's best friends: dogs&nbsp;
and cars. Director Chad Stahelski professed a love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.920
of dogs to Uproxx in 2019, saying his began as a&nbsp;
child in a household that raised Irish Wolfhounds.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.760
Stahelski's background inspired him to incorporate&nbsp;
dogs into the "John Wick" films. We see this first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.720
with the adorable baby beagle gifted to Wick&nbsp;
posthumously by his recently deceased wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.200
Helen. Her passing sets the stage for the&nbsp;
first film, and knowing her time was near,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.880
she scheduled Daisy's arrival for when&nbsp;
John would need her most. The puppy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.280
acts as an anchor for the grieving husband,&nbsp;
giving him a reason to keep going each day.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.520
Tragically, his tentative grip on becoming&nbsp;
whole slips away when Russian thugs break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.480
into his house, kill Daisy, and steal his&nbsp;
prized '69 Mustang — a wedding gift from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.960
Helen. It's heartbreaking to see the little&nbsp;
puppy die, so much so that even the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.360
tasked with "killing" the puppy on-set felt&nbsp;
the need to cuddle Daisy between takes. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.680
can't blame John for hunting down every person&nbsp;
affiliated with killing that innocent puppy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.000
and stealing his car. But, thankfully, in&nbsp;
real life the beagle is alive and well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.280
and has been living with a loving family&nbsp;
who have since renamed him "Wick."

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.440
Wick committed the ultimate unforgivable sin in&nbsp;
"John Wick Chapter 2," when he killed Santino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.920
D'Antonio on the neutral ground of "The&nbsp;
Continental." This set every bounty hunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.640
in the city after Wick, who attempted to lose&nbsp;
them in "Chapter 3 – Parabellum" by hiding out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.880
in a Central Park horse stable. In one&nbsp;
of his most creative and brutal moves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.880
Wick used one of the horses in the stables&nbsp;
as a weapon to fend off his pursuers.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.800
Placing the horse between himself and one&nbsp;
of the assassins, Wick encouraged the horse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.120
to back kick his opponent in the sternum. He&nbsp;
then slapped the horse's thigh so it followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.240
with a double-back kick to the guy's jaw, who&nbsp;
instantly drops like a sack of potatoes. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.320
Wick wrapped the horse's reins around his last&nbsp;
living opponent's neck, dragging him from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.960
stable behind him. It’s pretty intense, and&nbsp;
not a position anyone would ever want to be in.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.040
After escaping from the stable on&nbsp;
his death horse in "Parabellum,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.600
Wick is pursued by motorcyclist gangsters&nbsp;
through downtown Manhattan. In the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.480
Wick executes some amazing trick riding and&nbsp;
manages to vanquish all those in pursuit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.560
The motorcyclists get shot in the face&nbsp;
and crash into other cars pretty hard.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.600
Amazingly, Keanu Reeves performed&nbsp;
the stunt riding in this scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.680
skills he originally acquired while shooting the&nbsp;
2013 action and fantasy film "47 Ronin." In order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.240
to keep Reeves safe while filming the scene,&nbsp;
the crew set-up a custom wire-harness to act as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.280
a safety net in case he fell, or the horse took&nbsp;
off unexpectedly. Even with the safety harness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.080
Reeves had some close calls, which required him&nbsp;
to quickly pull his feet out of the stirrups and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.200
quote, “make [himself] small" so as to not to hit&nbsp;
anything. It sounds like Reeves' job, sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.840
is almost as dangerous as that of John Wick.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.120
In an ironic twist, the Russian&nbsp;
thug who orchestrated breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.040
into John's house was the son of&nbsp;
Russian crime boss Viggo Tarasov.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.200
As we learn in the original film, Wick and&nbsp;
Tarasov were former associates when he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.920
still an assassin, before Wick quit his life of&nbsp;
crime and killing to settle down and marry Helen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.040
So, after Viggo learns what his son has&nbsp;
done, he warns Iosef about Wick's, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.680
"focus, commitment, and sheer will,"&nbsp;
comparing him to the Russian Boogeyman.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.600
“They call him Baba Yaga.”

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.040
To give his heedless son an&nbsp;
idea of how dangerous Wick is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:41.120
Viggo tells Iosef about how he once killed three&nbsp;
men in a bar using only a pencil. This story is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.840
later repeated by Viggo's brother in "Chapter&nbsp;
2," but Stahelski wasn't content to just let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.000
his audience hear about this mind-boggling&nbsp;
feat. Later in "Chapter 2," Wick would bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.840
the legend to life by re-creating it&nbsp;
while escaping assassins in New York.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.800
Cornered by two men in a small café area, and&nbsp;
bleeding badly, the scene has Wick managing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.440
deflect one assassin's gunshot while grabbing&nbsp;
for a pencil lying by a server's notepad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.640
Then, he stabs one assailant in the&nbsp;
chest with the writing implement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.800
He uses a second pencil to vanquish the&nbsp;
other assassin and finish off the first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.600
The stabs are in places quite undelightful to&nbsp;
watch, eliciting squeamish squeals from many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.760
a-viewer. It can't help but make you look at&nbsp;
office supplies in a whole new, twisted light.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.600
In "Parabellum," John Wick adds to the list of&nbsp;
random inanimate objects he uses to kill people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.480
when he executes a fellow assassin using only&nbsp;
a book in the New York Public Library. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.880
would think a library would provide a safe&nbsp;
haven to read, but even there Wick can find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.840
no peace. Boban Marjanovic of the Dallas&nbsp;
Mavericks plays his towering opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:45.680
Ernest. He corners Wick down one of the abandoned&nbsp;
aisles and uses his long limbs to his advantage.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.560
To make matters worse, Marjanovic's character&nbsp;
has a knife and manages to strike true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.800
stabbing Wick in the shoulder after a few&nbsp;
deflected shots. Then, he grabs Wick and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.560
kicks him down the aisles while loudly and&nbsp;
ironically whispering for him to be quiet.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:00.160
“Shhh.”

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.120
The tides turn for Wick when he&nbsp;
manages to grab a particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.960
thick tome and hit Ernest squarely in&nbsp;
the crotch, throat, and face with it.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.840
Wick follows with a crushing death blow by placing&nbsp;
the book on a tabletop, smashing Ernest's face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.880
into the spine. He bends his neck and jaw back in&nbsp;
horrific angles accompanied by a sickening crack.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.840
Perhaps one of the wildest sequences in the&nbsp;
"John Wick" films takes place in "Parabellum."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.480
While on the run from ninjas, Wick makes a stop&nbsp;
inside an antique weapons store to arm himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.960
Old West style. His foes follow him inside&nbsp;
and they all begin volleying knives at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.320
another like Nerf darts, shattering countless&nbsp;
glass display cases and mirrors in the process.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.080
One of the ninjas gets stabbed in the&nbsp;
back by two knives thrown by his comrade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.840
before being stabbed in the groin and&nbsp;
a few other areas of the body by Wick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.840
Much more stabbing follows, culminating with&nbsp;
one of the last surviving ninjas getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.320
slowly shanked in the eye, which is super&nbsp;
uncomfortable to watch. To top it all off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.880
Wick finishes off the last ninja by throwing a&nbsp;
tomahawk into his skull from across the room.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.520
It may look like chaos, but this sequence was&nbsp;
intricately planned, and took a massive amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.720
of effort to complete. Stahelski told Polygon the&nbsp;
inspiration for this scene came from "The Good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.760
The Bad and The Ugly," when the big bad Tuco went&nbsp;
into a gun shop after-hours for supplies. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.880
starting with that spark of inspiration, Stahelski&nbsp;
expanded the scene to include an epic knife fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.520
akin to what he eloquently described as a,&nbsp;
quote, "snowball fight" — only far deadlier.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.640
Many scenes in the "John Wick" films use a&nbsp;
combination of CGI and practical effects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.480
create images that would otherwise be&nbsp;
impossible or incredibly dangerous to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.640
perform without digital aid. One such&nbsp;
example is an epic motorcycle chase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.640
scene in "Parabellum," when some of Zero's&nbsp;
ninjas are chasing Wick through Brooklyn.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.360
The scene gets even spicier when the ninjas pull&nbsp;
out katanas and attempt to slice and dice Wick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.760
while they're all riding around on two wheels.&nbsp;
Wick manages to turn their sword against them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.360
skewering ninjas like shish kabobs,&nbsp;
who then crash into each other in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.800
explosive domino effect. Despite&nbsp;
the hyper-realism of this scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.400
the riders hitting the pavement were&nbsp;
all edited into the scene with CGI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.120
layered into shots with real actors astride&nbsp;
motorcycle sled-rigs mounted in front of green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.000
screens. The cringe-inducing scenes go to show how&nbsp;
great of a job the visual effects team performed.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.720
Burning to death sounds like one of the most&nbsp;
painful ways to face your final moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.960
Even so, those are the consequences for being&nbsp;
complicit in the killing of John Wick's dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.760
and theft of his car. This fiery moment of&nbsp;
vengeance takes place in the original film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:27.360
when Wick finally locates Iosef Tarasov, thanks&nbsp;
to his dad Viggo sharing the information with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.840
the determined assassin. It seems there's no&nbsp;
honor among thieves, even when they're family.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.280
Wick tracks Iosef and his thugs&nbsp;
to a safehouse in Brooklyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.920
which suddenly becomes no longer safe, and&nbsp;
the thugs flee for their cars in hopes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.920
a quick escape. However, Wick remotely&nbsp;
blows up their cars before they can flee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.560
which explode in spectacular fashion. When did&nbsp;
he have time to plant bombs on multiple cars?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.040
Who cares! They go "boom" while Keanu Reeves&nbsp;
walks in slow-motion, and that's what matters.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.360
While trying to make one final desperate&nbsp;
escape, Wick shoots Iosef in the stomach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.240
before shooting him in the head while he's in&nbsp;
the process of protesting that it was just a dog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.160
Though, he actually got off easy compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.160
the guys who caught fire from the car&nbsp;
bombs. And a dog is never just a dog.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.200
John may not be able to bring his puppy&nbsp;
Daisy back, but at least he's able to reunite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.800
with his car. "Chapter 2" opens with Wick&nbsp;
infiltrating the chop shop of Abram Tarasov,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:25.040
uncle and brother to the late Iosef and Viggo,&nbsp;
respectively. With Wick's '69 Mustang in his shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.280
he fears the "Boogeyman" will come for his&nbsp;
car, and leave a path of collateral damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.920
in his wake. Abram fills an accomplice in on&nbsp;
the gritty details of Wick's capabilities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.120
their serious predicament. While Tarasov is&nbsp;
telling his story, Wick is right outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.920
working his way into the shop by quietly&nbsp;
taking out Abram's guys one at a time.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.800
One of those guys is on a smoke break when Wick&nbsp;
sneaks up behind him, and as Tarasov's voiceover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.520
plays on, describing Wick's focus, commitment,&nbsp;
and sheer will, Wick shoves the cigarette down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.400
the man's throat from the rear, then punches him&nbsp;
in the gut and while covering his mouth, forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.800
the smoldering cigarette down the man's throat.&nbsp;
One can only imagine how brutally that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.680
hurt. After decades of folks complaining that&nbsp;
Hollywood glamorizes smoking, here's one scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.880
that would seem to drive home another, albeit&nbsp;
rare, danger associated with nicotine addiction.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.240
Wick continues his quest to get his car&nbsp;
back with his next most brutal kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.560
which follows shortly after choking&nbsp;
Abram's thug with his own cigarette.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.200
The vengeful assassin sneaks up behind the next&nbsp;
baddie he crosses paths with at the chop shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:30.400
only this bad guy has made the&nbsp;
wise decision not to smoke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:35.520
So Wick does what he does best, and improvises&nbsp;
by taking him down and choking him with a wire.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.640
It seems like a very slow, painful way to die,&nbsp;
especially compared to how quickly a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:46.000
other deaths occur in the film via more "humane"&nbsp;
manners, such as point-blank gunshots. This may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.400
seem like a hard death to swallow, but many&nbsp;
follow in John Wick's bloody footsteps. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.720
least he gets his car back, even if he does&nbsp;
immediately trash it while making his getaway.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.040
It just goes to show with the right level of&nbsp;
focus, commitment, and sheer will, anything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.160
possible — even killing hundreds of people in&nbsp;
endlessly creative and savage ways. Stahelski&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:09.520
told the Los Angeles Times in 2019 that he and&nbsp;
his stunt team got bored easily with repetition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.680
so it will be interesting to see how they keep all&nbsp;
the killing feeling novel in the upcoming films.

